  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Damaging winds and widespread rain for this\nThanksgiving Day \n\nA widespread soaking rainfall is expected for this Thanksgiving\nas a strong coastal storm moves up the east coast. Rain now\ncovers nearly all of Pennsylvania with frequent gusts in the 20\n- 40mph range.\n\nMost of Central PA should see 1 to 2 inches of wind-whipped \nrain by the end of the day, though a few locations in favorable\nupslope areas on the east-facing slopes could see rainfall \namounts approach 3 inches. The latest guidance highlights a\npotential corridor of storm total rainfall amounts approaching \n3 inches.\n\nThe result of the long duration that the rainfall totals will\noccur over and the dry antecedent conditions means that this \nrain should largely be beneficial. That being said, drains\nblocked by drains and some other low lying areas could see some\nponding of water. Be careful and allow extra time and lower\nspeed for traveling to avoid hydroplaning, which can occur at\nspeeds as low as 30-35 mph. \n\nDamaging wind gusts are expected to be the primary hazard with \nthis system, particularly over the southeastern third of the CWA\nand for the Laurel Highlands this afternoon. SPC mesoanalysis \nshows that a 50 to 60 knot 850 mb easterly jet has formed on the\nnorth side of the developing low. This low- level jet continues\nto strengthen as the pressure gradient tightens between the \ndeepening low and the retreating high to the north, and most \nguidance continues to show a 60 to 90 knot jet moving over \nPennsylvania this afternoon. While a temperature inversion \naround the 900 mb level will prevent the strongest part of this \njet from mixing down to the surface, model soundings have been \nvery persistent in showing winds in the 50 to 60 knot range only\n500 meters above the surface. This wind shouldn't have too much\ntrouble making it down to the ground, resulting in surface wind\ngusts in the 45 to 55 mph range across much of Central PA. The \nLaurel Highlands and parts of the Lower Susquehanna Valley will \nlikely see gusts approaching 65 mph.\n\nA High Wind Warning continues for portions of the Lower \nSusquehanna Valley, as well as for Cambria and Somerset \nCounties through 00Z Friday. Additionally, a Wind Advisory is in\neffect for much of the rest of Central PA from through 00Z Fri,\noutside of a few counties across the north where winds may \nremain just below advisory criteria. \n\nSome guidance continues to suggest wind gusts in parts of \neastern Franklin County may exceed 60 mph, aided by downsloping,\nbut confidence in these winds occurring along with the small \narea in which these winds would occur has precluded warning \nissuance at this time. \n\nThe wind and rain will make for hazardous Thanksgiving travel \nconditions. Unfortunately, isolated to scattered power outages \nwill be possible, especially in the Lower Susquehanna Valley and\nLaurel Highlands where winds are likely to be the strongest.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Blustery and colder on Friday with rain \nshowers/elevation wintry mix\n\nColder air will gradually wrap back into Pennsylvania late \ntonight and Friday as the storm vertically stacks up across \nthe North-Central Appalachians. This should allow for snow to \nbegin mixing in especially over the higher terrain from the \nLaurel Highlands northward along the Allegheny Plateau into \nnorthern PA. Rain/snow mix during the day Friday beneath the \nstacked low transitions to more of a lake effect/upslope precip \npattern Friday night into Saturday as the system lifts into New \nEngland. As a back-end 805mb jet works its way into southwest \nPA, it will remain breezy into Saturday morning with west winds\ngusting 25-35 mph with strongest gusts in the Laurel Highlands.\nThereafter, winds will taper off and skies may brighten up a\nbit. Our next weather system arrives later Sunday with a brief\npatch of light rain showers.",
